            <ArticleTitle>Femoroacetabular impingement: A classic case of cam-type impingement in a 21-year-old soldier.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>781</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2484/rcr.v9i3.781</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We describe a case of femoroacetabular impingement (FAI) in a 21-year-old male U.S. Army Private. Pre-operative radiographs demonstrated a dysplastic bump at the right head-neck junction. The patient underwent arthroscopy and resection of the bump, resulting in a improved contour of the femoral head-neck junction. After standard recovery, he is now able to ambulate and flex his right hip without pain and has returned to full duty.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
